===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|savouren}}, {{m|enm|savour}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=sāvǒuren|pos=v|id=MED38656}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|savorer}}, {{m|xno|savourer}}, {{der|en|frm|savorer}}, {{m|frm|savourer}}, and {{der|en|fro|savorer}}, {{m|fro|savourer}} (modern {{cog|fr|savourer}}), from {{der|en|LL.|sapōrāre}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|LL.|sapōrō|t=to give flavour, make tasty; to give pleasure}}, from {{der|en|la|sapor}} (see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]])<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=savour {{!}} savor|pos=v|id=3626738198|date=December 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> + {{m|la|-ō|pos=suffix forming regular first-{{glossary|conjugation}} {{glossary|verb}}s}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|British spelling}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[detect]] (a [[flavor#Noun|flavour]] or [[taste#Noun|taste]], or [[food]] or [[drink#Noun|drink]]); to [[taste#Verb|taste]]; specifically, to [[enjoy]] or [[linger]] on (a flavour or taste, or food and drink); to [[relish#Verb|relish]].
##: {{ux|en|He closed his eyes so he could really '''savour''' his dessert.}}
## To [[give#Verb|give]] (food or drink) flavour; to [[flavor#Verb|flavour]], to [[season#Verb|season]].
##* {{RQ:d'Urfey Wit and Mirth|author=Samuel Wilde|volume=IV|chapter=[The Gard'ners Song.] The Second Part.|page=221|passage=What Flesh is fitting for Man to Eat, / Until our Herbs do '''savour''' the Meat?}}
##* {{RQ:Southey Brazil|part=1|chapter=XVIII|pages=616–617|pageref=617|passage=[S]alt they had none; the ashes of a species of palm was their substitute, and this could only be used for '''savouring''' food, not for curing it.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|chapter=w:Sir Gawain and the Green Knight|translator=[[w:J. R. R. Tolkien|J[ohn] R[onald] R[euel] Tolkien]]|title=Sir Gawain and the Green Knight, [[w:Pearl (poem)|Pearl]], and {{w|Sir Orfeo}}|edition=1st American|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton Mifflin Company]]|year=1975|section=stanza 37|page=47|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sirgawaingreen00tolk/page/47/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-395-21970-6|passage=[S]oups they served of many sorts, seasoned most choicely, / in double helpings, as was due, and divers sorts of fish; / some baked in bread, some broiled on the coals, / some seethed, some in gravy '''savoured''' with spices, / and all with condiments so cunning that it caused him delight.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To detect (an [[aroma]] or [[smell#Noun|smell]], especially an [[appealing#Adjective|appealing]] one); to [[smell#Verb|smell]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear Q1|act=IV|scene=ii|sig=H3|verso=1|page=70|passage=VViſedome and goodnes, to the vild ſeeme vild, / Filths '''ſauor''' but themſelues, vvhat haue you done?}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To enjoy (something) [[deeply]] or in a lingering [[manner#Noun|manner]]; to [[appreciate]], to [[delight in]], to relish.
###* {{RQ:Geneva Bible|Job|XX|12–14|folio=228|column=2|passage=VVhẽ vvickednes vvas ſvvete in his mouthe, & he hid it vnder his tongue, / And '''ſauoured''' it, and vvolde not forſake it, but kept it cloſe in his mouth, / ''Then'' his meat in his bovvels vvas turned: the gall of aſpes ''vvas'' in the middes of hĩ {{quote-gloss|him}}.}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=Andrew Mourant|title=Reinforced against Future Flooding|journal=[[w:Rail (magazine)|Rail]]|location=Peterborough, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Bauer Media Group|Bauer Media]]|date=26 August 2020|page=58|issn=0953-4563|oclc=999467860|passage=A journey along the Conwy Valley line is one to '''savour''' for aficionados of scenic railways.}}
### {{lb|en|chiefly in the negative}} To [[find#Verb|find]] (something) appealing; to appreciate, to [[like#Verb|like]].
###: {{synonyms|en|care for}}
###* {{RQ:Jonson Cynthia's Revels|act=III|scene=iiii|page=211|passage=Here, ſtalkes me by a proud, and ſpangled ſir, / That lookes three hand-fuls higher then his fore-top; / '''Sauours''' himſelfe alone, is onely kind / And louing to himſelfe: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:John Ford Broken Heart|sig=B|verso=1|sigend=B2|page=12|passage=Beauteous ''Penthea'' {{...}} is novv ſo yoak'd / To a moſt barbarous thraldome, miſery, / Affliction, that [s]he '''ſauors''' not humanity.}}
###* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|reprint=1875|page=27|passage=The man that met thee, is one ''World-'' / ''ly-Wiſeman'', and rightly is he ſo called; / partly, becauſe he '''ſavoureth''' only the / Doctrine of this world {{...}}}}
### {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[possess]] (a [[particular#Adjective|particular]], often [[negative#Adjective|negative]], [[quality#Noun|quality]]), especially a [[small#Adjective|small]] [[amount#Noun|amount]] of it; to be [[redolent]] or [[suggestive]] of (something).
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|edition=2nd|book=X|page=282|passage=No more be mention'd then of violence / Againſt our ſelves, and vvilful barrenneſs, / That cuts us off from hope, and '''ſavours''' onely / Rancor and pride, impatience and deſpite, / Reluctance againſt God and his juſt yoke / Laid on our Necks.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To be appealing to (a person, the [[sense#Noun|senses]], etc.).
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[experience#Verb|experience]], [[perceive]], or [[understand]] (something).
###* {{RQ:Warner Albions England|edition=5th|chapter=LXXVII|page=317|passage=By novv, perhaps, thou '''ſauoreſts'''{{sic|savorest}} ſome Godhead: yeat, is ods. / VVith paſte and preſent times doeſt dreame Pluralitie of Gods. / So did in deed the Monarchies, ſo Miſcreants novv not fevv: / But liſten here vvhat Gods they vvere, and learne them to eſchevv.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To give (something) a particular quality; to [[imbue]] with.
###* {{RQ:Lyly Euphues|part=1|chapter=Euphues|folio=7|passage=So theſe olde huddles hauing ouercharged their gorges with fancie, accompte all honeſt recreation meere folly, and hauinge taken a ſurfet of delyght, ſeeme now to '''ſauor''' it with deſpight.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} To give (something) an aroma or smell.
### {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} ''Followed by'' '''[[out#Preposition|out]]''': to detect or find (something).
### {{lb|en|obsolete|uncertain}} To have a [[suspicion]] of (something).
###* {{RQ:Marlowe Nashe Dido|sig=D2|verso=1|page=27|passage=Siſter, I ſee you '''ſauour''' of my vviles, / Be it as you vvill haue for this once, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} Of a [[thing]]: to [[give off]] a ([[specified#Adjective|specified]]) aroma or smell.
##* {{RQ:Latimer Notable Sermon|sig=A.iiii.|page=6|passage=[T]he ſaffrone bagge that hath bene full of ſaffron, or hath had ſaffron in it, doth euer after '''ſauoure''' and ſmel of the ſwete ſaffron that it conteyned: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=2|book=XXI|chapter=Of Parthenium, and the Medicinable Vertues that It hath|page=111|passage=[I]t [Parthenium] bringeth forth a vvhite floure, '''ſavouring''' like an apple, and having a bitter taſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Morris Earthly Paradise|part=IV|page=218|passage=The spilt blood '''savoured''' horribly, / Heart-breaking the dumb writhings were, / Unuttered curses filled the air; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|or|obsolete}} Of food or drink: to have a specified (especially appealing) flavour or taste.
##* {{RQ:Cornaro Herbert Temperance|page=8|passage=And I propoſed to try, vvhether thoſe [meats] that pleaſed my taſte brought me commoditie or discommoditie; and vvhether that Proverb, ''That vvhich '''ſavours''', is good and nouriſheth'', be conſonant to truth. This upon triall I found moſt falſe: {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To possess a particular (often negative) quality, especially a small amount of it; to be redolent or suggestive of.
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=V|scene=i|page=274|column=2|passage=[[w:Olivia (Twelfth Night)|''Ol''&#91;''ivia''&#93;]]. {{...}} Fetch ''[[w:Malvolio|Maluolio]]'' hither, / And yet alas, novv I remember me, / They ſay poore Gentleman, he's much diſtract. {{...}} Did he vvrite this? / ''Clo''[''wn'']. I {{quote-gloss|aye}} Madame. / [[w:Orsino (Twelfth Night)|''Du''&#91;''ke Orsino''&#93;]]. This '''ſauours''' not much of diſtraction.}}
###* {{RQ:Drayton Poly-Olbion|chapter=To the Generall Reader|sig=A|page=19|passage=In ſuch a ſeaſon, vvhen the Idle Humerous vvorld muſt heare of nothing, that either '''ſauors''' of Antiquity, or may avvake it to ſeeke after more, then dull and ſlothfull ignorance may eaſily reach vnto: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Preface|page=20|passage=In general I vvill only ſay, that I have vvritten nothing vvhich '''ſavours''' of Immorality or Profaneneſs; at leaſt, I am not conſcious to my ſelf of any ſuch intention.}}
###* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Addison|issue=262|date=31 December 1711|page=304|passage=But, notwithstanding I have rejected every thing that '''savours''' of party, every thing that is loose and immoral, and every thing that might create uneasiness in the minds of particular persons, I find that the demand of my papers has increased every month since their first appearance in the world.}}
###* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=I|book=I|chapter=The Hospitality of Allworthy; with a Short Sketch of the Characters of Two Brothers, a Doctor, and a Captain, who were Entertained by that Gentleman|page=53|passage=[S]uch Solicitations from Superiors alvvays '''ſavour''' very ſtrongly of Commands.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Joseph Bellamy|chapter=Discourse I. Shewing the Nature of the Divine Law, and wherein Consists a Real Conformity to It.|title=True Religion Delineated; or, Experimental Religion,{{nb...|as Distinguished from Formality on the One hand, and Enthusiasm on the Other, Set in a Scriptural and Rational Light. In Two Discourses, in which Some of the Principal Errors both of the Arminians and Antinomians are Confuted, the Foundation and Superstructure of Their Different Schemes Demolished, and the Truth as It is in Jesus, Explained and Proved. The Whole Adapted to the Weakest Capacities, and Designed for the Establishment, Comfort and Quickening of the People of God, in These Evil Times.}}|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher={{...|Printed and sold by}} S. Kneeland,{{nb...|in Queen-Street.}}|year=1750|section=section II (Shewing from what Motives True Love to God Takes Its Rise)|pages=53–54|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/truereligiondeli00bell/page/53/mode/1up|oclc=1204882185|passage=Be gone, thou impudent VVretch, to Hell, thy proper Place: thou art a Deſpiſer of my glorious Majeſty, and your Frame of Spirit '''ſavours''' of Blaſphemy.}}
###* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|page=234|passage=But the inſtitutions '''ſavour''' of ſuperſtition in their very principle; and they nouriſh it by a permanent and ſtanding influence.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Burroughs|chapter=Preface|title=The Breath of Life|location=Boston, Mass.&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton Mifflin Company]]{{nb...|The Riverside Press Cambridge [Mass.]}}|month=May|year=1915|pages=vi–vii|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/breathoflife0000unse/page/n9/mode/1up|oclc=1484959|passage=An explanation of life phenomena that '''savors''' of the laboratory and chemism repels me, and an explanation that '''savors''' of the theological point of view is  equally distasteful to me. I crave and seek a natural explanation of all phenomena upon this earth, but the word "natural" to me implies more than mere chemistry and physics.}}
